> You do this with constraints.  Something like:
>
> Time0 ~~ CV01*Time1 + CV03*Time3 + CV04*Time4
> Time1 ~~ CV13*Time3 + CV14*Time4
> Time3 ~~ CV34*Time4
>
> # constraints
> CV01 == CV13
> CV01 == CV34
>
> CV03 == CV01^2
> CV34 == CV01^2

The constraints idea is a good one, but autoregressive structure does not apply the power to the covariance but to the correlation.  So the covariance between Time 1 and 2 (or any adjacent times) would be $\sigma^2 \times \rho$ and the covariance between Time 1 and 3 (or any times twice as far apart) would be $\sigma^2 \times \rho^2$

I would add 2 things to Jeremy's advice:
First test whether the latent variances are the same, since simple autoregressive structure implies constant variance over time. If you use a marker variable (std.lv <http://std.lv> = FALSE), you constrain latent variances to equality and compare to the measurement-invariance model.  If you identify the model by fixing the variance at one occasion to 1, then you test the same hypothesis by fixing all latent variances to 1 and comparing to the measurement-invariance model.  These are equivalent models, so either way, if you cannot reject the null hypothesis that latent variances are similar over time, then you should use std.lv <http://std.lv> = TRUE so that the latent covariance estimates are actually latent correlations (which are what you actually want to apply those constraints to).
If you cannot reject the null about homogeneous latent variances, then you should not bother testing the more restrictive autoregressive structure.  Instead, you can test heterogeneous autoregressive structure (see Table 41.5 here <https://v8doc.sas.com/sashtml/stat/chap41/sect20.htm>, or chapter 7 in Singer & Willett's Applied Longitudinal Data Analysis), which allows variance to change over time.  Phantom constructs can be used to estimate a statistically equivalent model to the measurement-invariance model, but allow you to estimate latent correlations instead of covariances.  The trick is described in Todd Little's Longitudinal SEM book if you have it.  Basically, for each construct whose variance is not already fixed to 1, you specify that it loads onto a higher-order construct whose variance is fixed to 1.  For the model to be identified, you fix the lower-order (original) factor to have a variance of zero, and freely estimate the "factor loading" on the higher-order phantom construct.  For example:
Time0ph =~   Time0
Time0ph ~~ 1*Time0ph
Time0   ~~ 0*Time0

The free factor loading is therefore the latent SD (square-root of the previously estimated latent variance).  For this to work, you only estimate latent correlations among the phantoms (and any first-order factors whose variances are already fixed to 1).  You fix to zero all latent covariances among the lower-order factors that have phantom constructs, as well as covariances between those lower-order factors and any phantoms.  The resulting model should have the same df and test statistic as the measurement-invariance model; if not, then the specification is incorrect.

Once that is done, then you can add the constraints that Jeremy recommended (i.e., label the correlation estimates, and constrain all first-orders to have the same value, and for higher-orders to be raised to the power of how many occasions apart they are).

Regarding starting values, I would look at the less-constrained model in each model comparison as a guide (e.g., pick a first-order correlation starting value that is somewhere in the middle of the freely estimated ones).

Terrence D. Jorgensen
Postdoctoral Researcher, Methods and Statistics
Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam
