Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/lavaan/c/kkY-w10lOlE/m/4nEY-U1YBgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

> Using a multiplicative indicator, and using a multi-group model to test moderation can give exactly the same result.

Jeremy is right, and the difference between the same estimated parameter in 2 groups should be the estimated value of the slope of the interaction term if you used the single-group moderation approach.  This equivalence holds when the residual variances are equal across groups, which is something you can actually test in the multigroup model, but you can only assume in the single-group model.  The advantage of the multigroup approach to testing moderation is that you do not have to assume equality of residual variances (homoskedasticity) because you can let those estimates differ between groups.

I assume "they" are reviewers, or a dissertation committee?  Let them know that multigroup models DO allow you to test moderation.  When you freely estimate the same slopes across groups, that assumes there is moderation. If you constrain a slope to equality across groups, that assumes the effect is not moderated by that grouping variable.  You can compare the models with the lavTestLRT() function to obtain a likelihood ratio test of the null hypothesis of no-moderation.

If you want to show them that the single-group approach with an interaction term will produce the same test of moderation, you can enter product terms in the model syntax using the colon operator.  For example, if the effect of X on Y is moderated by the grouping dummy code G:

Y ~ X + G + X:G

